# Reflection Of The two Versions

In my initial design phase, I prioritized a stark, high-contrast aesthetic to represent my identity as a CCI student. My first version relied on decorative typography which, while visually bold, often presents challenges for legibility. By maintaining a pure black-and-white palette, I created a "minimalist" look, but I recognized that without proper structural spacing and font choice, the content could become difficult to parse for users with visual impairments or neurodivergent reading patterns.

To address these accessibility concerns in this version, I transitioned to the **Lexend** typeface via Google Fonts. Lexend was specifically designed to reduce visual stress and improve reading fluency, making it an ideal choice for digital interfaces. I also implemented a `max-width: 700px` on the `main` container. This ensures that the lines of text do not span the full width of the browser, which helps the reader’s eye track from the end of one sentence to the start of the next more efficiently. This structural constraint is vital for preventing "line-lost" during long-form reading.

Finally, I improved the page’s accessibility by using **semantic HTML5 tags** such as `<header>`, `<main>`, and multiple `<section>` blocks. This structure is not just for visual organization; it provides a roadmap for assistive technologies like screen readers, allowing users to jump between the "About Me," "Hobbies," and "Favourite Quote" sections easily. Even without changing my core color scheme, these technical refinements in typography and code structure have made the page significantly more inclusive and user-friendly.
